const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Agriculture | CBSE Class 10 Geography | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Geography Chapter 4 study notes on Agriculture. Types of farming (primitive subsistence, intensive subsistence, commercial/plantation), cropping seasons (Rabi, Kharif, Zaid), major food & cash crops (rice, wheat, millets, pulses, sugarcane, tea, coffee, cotton, jute), technological & institutional reforms, and Bhoodan-Gramdan with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Agriculture
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Geography Chapter 4</span>
      <h1>Agriculture</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Study India's agrarian backbone: three farming types, Rabi, Kharif, and Zaid cropping calendars, climatic requirements of staple food grains and commercial fiber crops, green revolution reforms, and Vinoba Bhave's Bhoodan movement.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Agricultural Systems, Cropping Seasons &amp; Crops</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🚜 Types of Farming Systems</h3>
          <p class="formula">From Primitive Jhumming to Capitalist Plantations</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. Primitive Subsistence Farming:</strong> Practised on small patches of land using primitive tools (hoe, dao, digging sticks) and family labor. Dependent on monsoon and natural soil fertility. Includes <strong>Slash and Burn (Jhumming)</strong> in Northeast (Assam, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland), Bewar/Dahiya in MP, Podu/Penda in AP.</li>
            <li><strong>2. Intensive Subsistence Farming:</strong> Practised in areas of high population pressure on land. Labor-intensive farming using high doses of biochemical inputs and irrigation to obtain high yields from small landholdings (caused by the right of inheritance subdividing plots).</li>
            <li><strong>3. Commercial Farming:</strong> Higher doses of modern inputs (HYV seeds, chemical fertilizers, insecticides) to obtain high productivity for market sale.</li>
            <li><strong>4. Plantation Agriculture:</strong> A type of commercial farming where a single crop is grown over a vast estate using capital-intensive inputs and migrant labor (e.g. Tea in Assam/North Bengal, Coffee in Karnataka, Rubber in Kerala).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🗓️ Three Cropping Seasons: Rabi, Kharif, Zaid</h3>
          <p class="formula">Agricultural Rhythm Aligned with Monsoons</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Rabi Crops (Winter):</strong>
              <br/>• Sown in winter from October to December; harvested in summer from April to June.
              <br/>• Important crops: <strong>Wheat, barley, peas, gram, mustard</strong>.
              <br/>• Success aided by western temperate cyclonic depressions bringing winter rains. States: Punjab, Haryana, Himachal, UP, Uttarakhand.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Kharif Crops (Monsoon):</strong>
              <br/>• Sown with onset of monsoon in June–July; harvested in September–October.
              <br/>• Important crops: <strong>Paddy (rice), maize, jowar, bajra, tur (arhar), moong, urad, cotton, jute, groundnut, soyabean</strong>.
              <br/>• In Assam, West Bengal, and Odisha, three crops of paddy are grown in a year: <em>Aus, Aman, and Boro</em>.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Zaid Season (Short Summer):</strong> Between Rabi and Kharif (March–June). Watermelon, muskmelon, cucumber, vegetables, and fodder crops.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌾 Major Crops of India &amp; Conditions</h3>
          <p class="formula">Climatic Requirements for Board Exams</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Rice (Kharif Staple):</strong> High temp (&gt; 25°C), high humidity, annual rainfall &gt; 100 cm. West Bengal, UP, Punjab, Haryana, deltaic plains. India is 2nd largest producer after China.</li>
            <li><strong>Wheat (Rabi Staple):</strong> Cool growing season, bright sunshine at ripening, 50–75 cm rainfall. Ganga-Satluj plains (Punjab, Haryana, UP, MP).</li>
            <li><strong>Millets (Coarse Grains with High Nutrition):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Jowar:</em> Rain-fed crop, 3rd most important food crop (Maharashtra, Karnataka).
              <br/>• <em>Bajra:</em> Sandy and shallow black soils (Rajasthan, UP, Gujarat).
              <br/>• <em>Ragi:</em> Rich in iron, calcium, roughage; dry regions on red/black/sandy soils (Karnataka, Tamil Nadu).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Pulses:</strong> India is largest producer and consumer. Major source of protein in vegetarian diet. Leguminous crops (except Arhar) fix atmospheric nitrogen, restoring soil fertility. (MP, Rajasthan, Maharashtra, UP).</li>
            <li><strong>Cash &amp; Fiber Crops:</strong> Sugarcane (hot &amp; humid 21–27°C, 75–100 cm rain), Tea (subtropical, frost-free, well-drained deep loamy soil rich in humus), Coffee (Arabica variety from Yemen grown in Baba Budan Hills/Nilgiris), Cotton (black soil, high temp, light rain, 210 frost-free days), Jute (Golden fiber, well-drained floodplains, West Bengal).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>📜 Institutional Reforms &amp; Bhoodan</h3>
          <p class="formula">Green Revolution, White Revolution &amp; Land Reforms</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Institutional Reforms:</strong> Collectivization, consolidation of holdings (Chakbandi), cooperation, and abolition of the Zamindari system were given priority in the First Five Year Plan.</li>
            <li><strong>Technological Reforms:</strong> Green Revolution (HYV seeds, chemical fertilizers) and White Revolution (Operation Flood).</li>
            <li><strong>Comprehensive Land Development Programme (1980s &amp; 90s):</strong> Crop insurance against drought, flood, cyclone; establishment of Grameen banks and cooperative credit societies; Kissan Credit Card (KCC) and Personal Accident Insurance Scheme (PAIS); special weather bulletins and MSP announcements.</li>
            <li><strong>Bhoodan - Gramdan (Blood-less Revolution):</strong> Initiated by <strong>Vinoba Bhave</strong>. At Pochampalli (AP), Ram Chandra Reddy donated 80 acres to 80 landless villagers (Bhoodan). Zamindars later donated entire villages (Gramdan).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

